import{_ as a,o as t,c as i,ak as n}from"./chunks/framework.B8jO0odJ.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"Chainy manuscript working draft","description":"Updated October 23, 2026 for adopted GDD v2. Sean owns authored manuscript content; Christian supplies technical evidence, Luis art sources and Miguel UI evidence. This is a d","frontmatter":{"title":"Chainy manuscript working draft","description":"Updated October 23, 2026 for adopted GDD v2. Sean owns authored manuscript content; Christian supplies technical evidence, Luis art sources and Miguel UI evidence. This is a d","source":"docs/manuscript-working-draft.md","category":"Research & evaluation","sourceUpdated":"2026-10-23T16:51:22+08:00","outline":[2,3]},"headers":[],"relativePath":"content/docs/manuscript-working-draft.md","filePath":"content/docs/manuscript-working-draft.md"}'),s={name:"content/docs/manuscript-working-draft.md"};function o(r,e,c,d,l,p){return t(),i("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[n('<div><h1 id="chainy-manuscript-working-draft">Chainy manuscript working draft<a class="header-anchor" href="#chainy-manuscript-working-draft" aria-label="Link to this section">​</a></h1><p>Updated October 23, 2026 for adopted GDD v2. Sean owns authored manuscript content; Christian supplies technical evidence, Luis art sources and Miguel UI evidence. This is a drafting foundation, not a completed school submission.</p><p>Use the <a href="/Chainy-Docs/content/docs/manuscript-evidence-map.html">official outline</a> for required numbering and appendices. Verified literature, actual prototype evidence, study details and results must still be supplied.</p><h2 id="chapter-i--introduction">Chapter I — Introduction<a class="header-anchor" href="#chapter-i--introduction" aria-label="Link to this section">​</a></h2><h3 id="project-context">Project context<a class="header-anchor" href="#project-context" aria-label="Link to this section">​</a></h3><p>Chainy is intended for learners aged 12–17, especially learners in low-income and remote communities with limited technology access. It introduces selected foundational Web3, Blockchain and artificial intelligence concepts through an offline Android educational game. The audience and access priorities are project decisions; they do not establish measured needs at the pilot school without supporting evidence.</p><h3 id="purpose-and-description">Purpose and description<a class="header-anchor" href="#purpose-and-description" aria-label="Link to this section">​</a></h3><p>The proposed system is an offline island-restoration adventure. Learners help residents, earn fictional currency, review a lantern purchase and inspect/repair the same record in the Blockchain area. Contextual questions support reasoning. Unity runs exploration and interactions; SQLite stores local profiles, quests, inventory, balances, ledger and progress. Teacher provisioning/collection belongs to a later service scope, not the November 10 gameplay core.</p><p>Restoration actions provide context; Quiz checks reasoning, while later Wordle can reinforce vocabulary and BlockMatch can practice relationships. Each activity requires a stated objective and an explanation of its result. Whether these activities support learning must be evaluated rather than assumed from engagement or completion.</p><h3 id="objectives">Objectives<a class="header-anchor" href="#objectives" aria-label="Link to this section">​</a></h3><p>The general objective is to design, develop and evaluate an offline educational game introducing selected emerging-technology concepts to the intended learners.</p><p>Proposed specific objectives are to deliver the adopted W01–W04 and B01–B03/demo B07 core chapter; provide accurate explanations and feedback; retain separate local profiles and reliable progress; verify operation on selected Android devices; and evaluate the delivered system using the school&#39;s approved procedure.</p><h3 id="scope-and-delimitation">Scope and delimitation<a class="header-anchor" href="#scope-and-delimitation" aria-label="Link to this section">​</a></h3><p>The first version is single-player and English-first. The GDD labels CORE, STRETCH, FULL and LATER SERVICE explicitly; all twelve concepts remain in the full design, not all promised as hands-on beta activities. A learner code identifies a local profile; it does not authenticate a person or restore another device&#39;s save. Teacher collection is separate from offline gameplay.</p><p>V2 introduces a distinct evidence-based Blockchain repair mechanic; it is not a renamed BlockMatch. A01–A03 is conditional stretch and must use an actual small local classifier with separate training/practice/final cases. Scripted assistant and supervised dispatch activities remain full-scope work. Standalone Wordle and BlockMatch are deferred from beta. Device support and learning effectiveness remain subject to actual tests.</p><h3 id="significance">Significance<a class="header-anchor" href="#significance" aria-label="Link to this section">​</a></h3><p>The intended contribution is an accessible offline learning experience, with clear activities for learners, supervised use for teachers and documented implementation/evaluation evidence for the team. The final manuscript must support these claims with actual findings and acknowledge the limits of its study.</p><h2 id="chapter-ii--literature-and-technical-background">Chapter II — Literature and technical background<a class="header-anchor" href="#chapter-ii--literature-and-technical-background" aria-label="Link to this section">​</a></h2><p>Sean must build a verified literature review on game-based learning, vocabulary/concept activities, introductory technology education, offline mobile access, usability and evaluation. For each study record its participants, method, findings, limitations and relevance. Do not claim novelty or effectiveness from the game concept alone.</p><p>Use Christian&#39;s <a href="/Chainy-Docs/content/docs/technical-design.html">technical design</a> to explain the project&#39;s component responsibilities and <a href="/Chainy-Docs/content/docs/design/database-schema.html">database reference</a> for local records. Technical documentation supports implementation facts; it does not substitute for evidence about educational outcomes.</p><p>Keep terms consistent: a world is a subject area; a quest is a goal/state progression; a contextual Quiz supports an action; a profile is local; progress is stored current state. Discovered, Explained, Practiced and Demonstrated are game evidence labels, not validated mastery. Define score, attempt and percentage using Christian and Sean&#39;s agreed data meanings.</p><h2 id="chapter-iii--design-and-methodology">Chapter III — Design and methodology<a class="header-anchor" href="#chapter-iii--design-and-methodology" aria-label="Link to this section">​</a></h2><p>The development approach is to finish and test a small complete increment, then reuse its shared systems. The first complete increment is W01–W04 with reliable local save/reload, followed by B01–B03/demo B07. Integration and device testing precede Cryptita Plays community beta testing on November 10. AI is conditional stretch; Wordle/BlockMatch and teacher services are deferred.</p><p>The system has presentation, game-logic and data layers. UI actions pass through focused handlers/services; typed events notify independently owned components. One activity owns active input; dialogue blocks world controls. Progress Manager coordinates records through Database Manager and SQLite. UI Manager presents state and save outcomes.</p><p>The evaluation must distinguish technical operation, usability and learning evidence. Respondents, sampling, sample size, approved instruments, assistance policy and analysis method remain for school/adviser confirmation. A pre/post procedure is a recommendation if the approved study measures learning change; it is not an already approved design or completed session.</p><p>Required source material: <a href="/Chainy-Docs/content/docs/requirements/product-requirements.html">requirements</a>, <a href="/Chainy-Docs/content/docs/game-design.html">GDD</a>, <a href="/Chainy-Docs/content/docs/design/app-flow.html">flow</a>, <a href="/Chainy-Docs/content/docs/design/database-schema.html">data</a>, <a href="/Chainy-Docs/content/docs/hardware-validation.html">devices</a>, <a href="/Chainy-Docs/content/docs/kanban-plan-draft.html">task plan</a>, <a href="/Chainy-Docs/content/docs/qa-evaluation.html">evaluation</a>. Cite actual build/screens/tests when describing the implemented prototype. Preserve the school&#39;s unresolved wording “ISO 2010” until its instrument is identified.</p><h2 id="chapters-ivv--results-and-conclusions">Chapters IV–V — Results and conclusions<a class="header-anchor" href="#chapters-ivv--results-and-conclusions" aria-label="Link to this section">​</a></h2><p>Write results only from actual preliminary/system evaluation and technical tests. Record the delivered build, participant context, usable and missing records, analysis method and limitations. Keep raw participant records and identifying documents in the approved restricted location.</p><p>Conclusions must answer the objectives to the extent supported by those results. Completion and enjoyment alone do not prove learning gains; a simple observed pre/post change does not by itself establish causation.</p><p>The fixed pre-final is November 21, 2026. The community beta replaces the Marinduque school event; adviser acceptance of community feedback as required pilot-area evidence still needs confirmation. Plan actual community beta analysis November 11–13, manuscript assembly November 14–16 and rehearsal November 17–20. Missing required evidence remains a delivery issue, not a result to invent.</p></div>',1)])])}const m=a(s,[["render",o]]);export{u as __pageData,m as default};
